I was hoping for something like this (draft status)
```
<name> ::= 'DT_' <node-id> [ '_' <suffix> ];

<init-id> ::= [A-Z]
<id-char> ::= [A-Z0-9]
<decimal> ::= [0-9]+ ;
<hexadecimal> ::= [0-9a-f]+ ;

<identifier> ::= <init-id> <id-char>* ;
<long-identifier> ::= <identifier> ( '_' <identifier> )* ;

<node-name> ::= <long-identifier> ;
<node-label> ::= <long-identifier> ;
<unit-address> ::= hexadecimal [ '_' <unit-address> ] ;

<property-name> ::= <long-identifier> ;

# An encoded vendor,model string from a "compatible" property
<compatible> ::= <long-identifier> ;

# A sequence meant to identify a specific node in the devicetree.
# Used as a prefix for node properties and to represent the node
# itself.
<node-id> ::= <node-alias> | <node-compat> | <node-inst> | <node-label> | <node-path> ;

# This name is used to reference a generic capability such as an LED
# or button.
<node-alias> ::= 'ALIAS_' + <alias> ;

# A property from the /aliases node
<alias> ::= <property-name>

# This name is used in driver instantiation
<node-inst> ::= 'INST_' <decimal> '_' <compatible> ;

# This name is used to reference by SOC-specific identifier
<node-label> ::= 'NODELABEL_' <long-identifier> ;

# This name is used to reference by full devicetree path
<node-path> ::= 'PATH_' <path> ;
<path> ::= ; # **TBD**

# Some old thing we probably want to get rid of.
<node-legacy> ::= [ <bus> '_' ] <compatible> '_' <unit_address> ;
<bus> ::= <long-identifier> ;

<suffix> ::= <property-suffix> ;

# Used to identify a property value (or value component)
<property-suffix> ::= <property-name> [ <ordinal> ] ;

```

@pabigot and any others interested in discussing.

I want to start with the grammar as defined in v2.2.0-rc2, and tackle `node-id` (used by `property-macro`) first as a warmup for the process:

https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/v2.2.0-rc2/doc/guides/dts/macros.bnf

I've created a [dt-macros-ng branch](https://github.com/mbolivar/zephyr/tree/dt-macros-ng) in my fork, which contains this commit at time of writing with proposed `node-id` changes that keep the old names around, but introduce new (hopefully better) ones as well:

https://github.com/mbolivar/zephyr/commit/3a7dc57f38afa4155f5f7a9cc4e4fdf777574a53

Using these conveniently would require some macro magic à la @galak.

Comment welcome. Here are some notes on collisions in the meantime:

Node identifiers from two different namespaces (like "alias" and
"inst") cannot collide, since they have different prefixes.

"inst" macros for devices from two different vendors cannot collide as
long as compatibles follow rules in vendor-prefixes.txt, since those
lead to unique "VND_" prefixes (even in cases like "u-blox" where
"vnd" is not purely alphanumeric).

"inst" collisions between compatibles "vnd,device" and
"generic-device" (like "gpio-keys", "mmio-sram", etc.) cannot occur if
the "generic-device" namespace is properly managed, which I think we
can assume the maintainers will ensure. Similar comments apply to
collisions between two different "generic-device" compatibles.

"alias" collisions can occur only for two aliases differing only by
case, like this:

```
aliases {
	foo = &foo1;
	FOO = &foo2;
};
```

This follows because alias names may contain alphanumeric and "-"
(dash) characters only (at least per the devicetree specification).
The "foo vs. FOO" problem isn't worth solving IMO: the user should
just fix their devicetree to avoid it.

"nodelabel" collisions are only possible under the same conditions as
"alias" collisions, for a similar reason. Per specification, labels
can only contain "_" (underscore) and alphanumeric characters. The
"foo vs. FOO" problem isn't worth solving here, either, IMO.

"path" collisions are the likeliest of all, because of various types
of ambiguity that can occur when converting a path to a c-ident. For
example, the leaf nodes in this devicetree all have "path" prefix
`DT_p_path_FOO_BAR_BAZ`, and all names are legal per specification
AFAICT:

```
/ {
	foo {
		bar@baz {};
		bar {
			baz {};
		};
	};
	foo-bar-baz {};
	foo_bar_baz {};
	foo,bar,baz {};
	foo.bar+baz {};
};
```

We need to decide how seriously to take these problems.

We may want to send a patch to the devicetree specification which
explicitly recommends against such situations, at least at the same
level in the tree (so the answer to `foo-bar-baz` vs. `foo_bar_baz` at
least might just be "don't do that!").

If that patch is merged, we could then separate path components with a
special character (like "s" for "slash" as previously suggested by
@pabigot). Then the leaf nodes in this tree:

```
/ {
	foo {
		bar@baz {};
		bar {
			baz {};
		};
	};
	foo-bar-baz {};
};
```

Would have the following "path" prefixes (in the respective order they
are defined in the DTS):

- `DT_n_path_s_FOO_s_BAR_BAZ`
- `DT_n_path_s_FOO_s_BAR_s_BAZ`
- `DT_n_path_s_FOO_BAR_BAZ`

